{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"f09d6245b3db8e3a","type":"text","text":"==Exorcists bonds==","x":-32,"y":25,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"98acb3c61d50e684","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> (Addendum: for this campaign bonds can be applied from operative to operative *and* from worm to worm, for the sake of clarification in the following rules ","x":-92,"y":-300,"width":371,"height":189},
		{"id":"7d80e507ffea2af5","type":"text","text":"==Deep in my heart==","x":460,"y":25,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"e81c57a94296c35b","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> CAIN unabashedly touts it as part of their centuries-spanning dogma: ==Exorcists are not human==. So it was written, long ago, in the Covenant of Worms, a medieval parchment text said to now adorn the central meeting room of AUTHORITY, set in a glass case in the wall.","x":391,"y":-424,"width":389,"height":249},
		{"id":"2d9e885a77739437","type":"text","text":"**Connections will get you killed *or* save your sorry ass one of these days, I tell ya now!**\n\n**Sure you are not allowed anything *too* interpersonal to flourish before Admin tries to get your ass in a silver plate by adding yourself more vulnerable points for the enemy to exploit, but hey...**","x":900,"y":-424,"width":360,"height":249},
		{"id":"eafcf75df72b76bf","type":"text","text":"**...If a upper guy asks me to prove that you were breaking one of their silly rules...**","x":937,"y":-700,"width":287,"height":122},
		{"id":"57967824abd3b6b1","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> However, while the letters of that mummified goatskin scroll writhe in the minds of all who serve CAIN, they do not change this fact:","x":424,"y":-744,"width":323,"height":210},
		{"id":"11f90eb4806bccc3","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==Exorcists long for connection to one another==. Each of them possesses that exceptionally human weakness. That font of pathos, of love and of loss.","x":399,"y":-1080,"width":374,"height":180},
		{"id":"44b18520bae1d407","type":"text","text":"**...I was *looking the other way,* if you catch my drift, hey?**","x":944,"y":-997,"width":272,"height":97},
		{"id":"7aa2c865cc42a705","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> It is precisely for this reason that many Exorcists serve CAIN at all. The chance to be with one another, to be seen, held, needed, no matter how briefly and at what cost of great self-destruction. To their battered hearts, even the disdainful treatment of CAIN is acknowledgement.","x":395,"y":-1500,"width":383,"height":240},
		{"id":"b36dafeb0adbc0e8","type":"text","text":"**Forget the rules! go live your shitty existence in duty, out of duty, fucking anywhere and whatever you want! YOU CAN BE DEAD ALEADY BY TOMORROW FOR FUCKS SAKE!**\n\n**Steal shit, take photos of your new friends when still around, make someone angry enough to consider them your short-lived rival once they are gone!**\n\n***GO*** \n***AND***\n***EXIST***","x":883,"y":-1544,"width":394,"height":328},
		{"id":"41b0a8b9278bbc1f","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> There is a saying:\n> \n> “Hungry dogs will eat shit.”","x":433,"y":-1809,"width":306,"height":129},
		{"id":"bf42d7b4c39d213d","type":"text","text":"**Tomorrows are not free and neither a common thing around here**\n\n**Seize life by the fucking spine AND MAKE YOUR CORPSE MEMORABLE FOR THOSE AFTER YOU TO REMEMBER FOR THE YEARS TO COME COMRADE**","x":878,"y":-1834,"width":399,"height":180},
		{"id":"08ff0a316151662e","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==Exorcist Bonds== are a way for Exorcists to improve their abilities through personal connection, while at the same time risking greater suffering should something happen to their closely held peers. ","x":-93,"y":260,"width":373,"height":220},
		{"id":"35f33d39e679974e","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> When an Exorcist takes an action that would ==grant another Exorcist Agenda XP and that Exorcist is in the scene with them==, these two Exorcists can create a shared three-slash ==bond talisman==. Slash it each time:","x":-98,"y":620,"width":383,"height":220},
		{"id":"3ca92944d6811306","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ● One of these Exorcists ==sets up== the other.\n> \n> ● Both of these Exorcists are part of a\n> teamwork action.\n> \n> ● One of these Exorcists sacrifices\n> something meaningful for the other.","x":-660,"y":880,"width":420,"height":220},
		{"id":"7dfed96487244055","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> When the bond talisman is filled, these\n> Exorcists gains a ==bond==, based around\n> beliefs they each have about one another.\n> Here’s an example:\n","x":-98,"y":899,"width":401,"height":182},
		{"id":"05a8753116b41503","type":"file","file":"Cain-Glory unto them-summary/Pasted image 20261021124644.png","x":-97,"y":1160,"width":400,"height":133},
		{"id":"76d56bc1b8cdc261","type":"text","text":"==Bond effects==","x":521,"y":959,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"9eb6a99c7cd7d375","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> The benefits of having Bonds are as follows:\n> ","x":-35,"y":1400,"width":276,"height":131},
		{"id":"98a78bc24e5390f6","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ● If an Exorcist has at least two Bonds,\n> when entering a Sin’s Palace, they\n> automatically gain ==1 pathos==. If they have\n> a Bond with every member of the group,\n> or at least one S Bond, they also ==gain a==\n> ==Psyche Burst.==","x":-98,"y":1680,"width":401,"height":220},
		{"id":"c595a37905dd9eaa","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ● Bonds can be progressed from level B\n> to A and then to S by the same process\n> as gaining a Bond.","x":-632,"y":1720,"width":392,"height":140},
		{"id":"1d7127ca05597d5c","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ● Bonds can be ==broken== to create pathos\n> at the cost of sin. Level B Bonds grant 2\n> sin and 1 pathos when broken, to be\n> divided amongst the Exorcists to whom\n> the Bond belonged. \n","x":-339,"y":2060,"width":442,"height":200},
		{"id":"e11304d4e2dba5da","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ● Higher level bonds create more extreme\n> effects when broken: ==1d3 sin and 2==\n> ==pathos== for A Bonds, ==1d3+1 sin and 3==\n> ==pathos== for S Bonds","x":240,"y":2072,"width":480,"height":177},
		{"id":"c689fcd8d6105cda","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ● A Bond can only be progressed once per hunt, and not in the same hunt that the Bond was created.","x":820,"y":1720,"width":360,"height":143},
		{"id":"c03c8eefd2344004","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ● Progressing a Bond grants the involved Exorcists 1 XP each.","x":400,"y":1718,"width":290,"height":142},
		{"id":"a3d3ea8354d32ba3","type":"text","text":"==Progressed bonds== ","x":-1020,"y":1760,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"5613016151bb80b7","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> A and S Bonds should be interpreted as the connection between characters growing ==more intense==, not necessarily more positive or affectionate. For instance, this is a way a bond could progress:","x":-1560,"y":1679,"width":361,"height":222},
		{"id":"9b97e50b786a1a6d","type":"file","file":"Cain-Glory unto them-summary/Pasted image 20261021143045.png","x":-1579,"y":1360,"width":400,"height":232},
		{"id":"12c5b6fa7d739bcc","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> However, this is also a possible progression of a bond:","x":-1544,"y":2040,"width":330,"height":124},
		{"id":"ea7c5ff94249351c","type":"file","file":"Cain-Glory unto them-summary/Pasted image 20261021143206.png","x":-1579,"y":2260,"width":400,"height":243},
		{"id":"1d8b0f614def603e","type":"text","text":"==Breaking bonds==","x":-2049,"y":1914,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"1de7afac2ec16db1","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> The act of breaking a Bond does not have to mean that the characters betray each other or go from friends to enemies.","x":-2540,"y":1861,"width":318,"height":167},
		{"id":"b18ce9602484c92a","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> It means that these characters now have to rebuild their relationship from the ground up. Whatever it was before has ended.","x":-2553,"y":2164,"width":345,"height":176},
		{"id":"711332b698591983","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> The way players agree to distribute sin and pathos between their Exorcists when they break their bond should reflect the fiction of their confrontation.","x":-3040,"y":2153,"width":359,"height":198},
		{"id":"b37344fd6764df63","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> As a shorthand, characters who feel guilty or vengeful when breaking a bond should get the most sin, and ones who feel hurt or vulnerable should get the most pathos. These are intentionally not mutually exclusive.","x":-3035,"y":1789,"width":349,"height":239},
		{"id":"fedda06de44c5479","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> To complicate the lives of Exorcists, an Admin may (within reason) declare certain actions impossible to take without breaking specific Bonds, like treason, murder, or leaving someone behind.","x":-3041,"y":1440,"width":361,"height":220},
		{"id":"01967d37b3b754e2","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Bonds can be broken at any time by characters’ narrative actions. If multiple Bonds break in one scene, ==only the highest level Bond== (choose one if tied) generates sin and pathos.","x":-3540,"y":1451,"width":359,"height":198},
		{"id":"de8a24fe267f43b7","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Breaking a Bond grants the involved Exorcists 1 XP each.","x":-3512,"y":1760,"width":303,"height":113},
		{"id":"9bf02522557a1662","type":"text","text":"==Duality break==","x":-1096,"y":1198,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"aa45bfb986ddc09f","type":"text","text":"==SELF AND OTHER== ","x":-1110,"y":925,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"cfb5403ce01e7e2e","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> The ability to distinguish between what is and isn’t you is one of the primary requirements to develop a stable sense of personhood.","x":-1735,"y":700,"width":314,"height":192},
		{"id":"d780886b4b2e3ed5","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> And yet, this central dogma of the self is always under assault, by non-dualist yogis and lovesick teenagers alike. It seems that somewhere within the most intense spheres of human emotion there is a latent desire for the destruction of the self, the abolition of boundaries, the merging into one.","x":-1775,"y":1004,"width":393,"height":254},
		{"id":"4bd19e7503533715","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> After all, to be yourself is so cold and lonely","x":-2260,"y":1320,"width":302,"height":125},
		{"id":"fb4ba8bc0c26df27","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Tightly bonded Exorcists are brimming with an aggressive voltage of psychic energy, two poles of psyche galvanised to leap at each other and crush the barriers of psychological self-preservation in their way.","x":-2272,"y":960,"width":326,"height":235},
		{"id":"c45863c8ae4bcb49","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==Exorcists with an S-level Bond== suffer an alternative version of Sin Overflow, called ==Duality Break.==","x":-2253,"y":636,"width":288,"height":160},
		{"id":"1bb59c95d63d4c62","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> If one side of an S-level Bond would go into Sin Overflow, both Exorcists do. This has the following effects:","x":-2272,"y":220,"width":332,"height":158},
		{"id":"1ebe094ac15e4813","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ● To ==give up==, both Exorcists must choose to give up.","x":-2760,"y":244,"width":311,"height":110},
		{"id":"172b2133615e48bb","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ● If they give up or fail, the bodies of both Exorcists ==merge== into a particularly horrendous and powerful ==Chimeric Imago==.","x":-2760,"y":500,"width":310,"height":165},
		{"id":"e55bb41446f8cbde","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ● So long as one Exorcist resists, the test is passed for both. However, unless both Exorcists resist, they each suffer 1d6 Stress.","x":-2744,"y":-100,"width":280,"height":202},
		{"id":"10e425f257757ea9","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ● When overcoming a Duality Break, the surviving Exorcists both gain a Sin Mark. Only roll for them once, both gain this result. These ==Conjoined Sin Marks== are either the exact same sin mark, or in some way complete each other (e.g. a right wing on one Exorcist and a left one on the other).","x":-1660,"y":162,"width":368,"height":274},
		{"id":"5e1fb7d0f1cc325e","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ● Conjoined Sin Marks grant one of six additional abilities to their bearers (see next page).","x":-1627,"y":-200,"width":303,"height":146},
		{"id":"a584c415285c08cf","type":"text","text":"==Conjoined sin marks==","x":-1600,"y":-460,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"0363942f888e0bb2","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> When two Exorcists gain Conjoined Sin Marks, they both roll six-sided dice until the number that comes up is the same. The Marks grant the additional ability tied to that number from the list below.","x":-2180,"y":-529,"width":388,"height":198},
		{"id":"5713cd4b14e599cc","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==1: Dirty little secret==\n> \n> The Exorcists gain a single additional Sin box that is shared between them. When gaining sin, either Exorcist can choose to make use of it. While it is filled, the Exorcists are aware when the other is lying, flustered or upset. It can only be cleared if neither Exorcist has other Sin boxes to clear","x":-2827,"y":-561,"width":447,"height":263},
		{"id":"20018ca3872adcbc","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==2: The writing's on the wall==\n> \n> Writing applied to either Exorcist’s skin also appears on the skin of the other, in the same place (unless that body part is absent). Only three words or one crude drawing transfer legibly this way per scene. This can be raised to ten words or one somewhat detailed drawing if both Exorcists pay 1 Stress by writing in their blood.","x":-3540,"y":-555,"width":500,"height":252},
		{"id":"3e54424a97affeac","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==3: Us against the world==\n> \n> Abilities used by the Exorcists that target one another have +1 CAT. The Exorcists cannot add benefit to Teamwork if they are not both included, and they cannot gain the benefits of resting if they are not together.","x":-4040,"y":-792,"width":395,"height":231},
		{"id":"56b5460639c9ef26","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==4: Birds of a feather==\n> \n> The Exorcists can never be separated as a consequence of a failed roll. Supernatural abilities of Sins can still separate them.","x":-3540,"y":-960,"width":418,"height":204},
		{"id":"776cde47e7c4096b","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==5: Twin swords of the deva==\n> \n> The Exorcists can only set each other up. When they do, the Exorcist being set up gains +1D and decreased difficulty, but cannot gain decreased risk.","x":-2806,"y":-976,"width":406,"height":236},
		{"id":"daaac896c9d78858","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==6: Your angel and your devil==\n> \n> Your joint Sin Mark creates a ==Mascot==, a small psychic eidolon that embodies the bond between the two of you. It cannot be perceived by regular humans. It can: \n> \n> ● Psychically convince regular humans that you two shouldn’t be separated. \n> \n> ● Ferry small mundane items between the two of you (a box of matches, a note, a cell phone, a small pair of scissors), at the speed of a paper plane. \n> \n> Any damage destroys a Mascot, but it will re-manifest whenever either Exorcist gains Sin.","x":-2180,"y":-1065,"width":540,"height":415},
		{"id":"3c5478b0a3c41b30","type":"text","text":"==Downtime bonding==","x":800,"y":959,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"4f73adace731d1b8","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Exorcists aren’t limited to hunts when it comes to developing bonds with one another - although the rampant adrenaline and psychic activation of the Hunt is a potent catalyst for it.","x":1240,"y":903,"width":500,"height":173},
		{"id":"8f75d59bf26eea77","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> CASTLE is aware that Exorcists should be kept busy while not on hunts, ideally in a way that is productive to their profession: sports and self-study are encouraged, purely recreational pursuits are begrudgingly permitted depending on the facility and the moving of scrip.","x":1276,"y":540,"width":428,"height":220},
		{"id":"fcd938319165191c","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> When Exorcists finish a hunt with an incomplete Bond Talisman, downtime activities at CASTLE’s facilities can be a way to complete the Bond Talisman anyway.","x":1900,"y":550,"width":375,"height":201},
		{"id":"92f9a524c89a2602","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Think of this as a way to follow up on an interest Exorcists have taken in each other during a hunt, but weren’t able to act on at the time.","x":1900,"y":903,"width":379,"height":181},
		{"id":"4b2418fb67b70b05","type":"text","text":"==Exorcists activities==","x":1964,"y":1303,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"9ca59dac4c5a48f8","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Between hunts, each Exorcist can choose ==one== activity to pursue from these options, or ones introduced later in the book.","x":1309,"y":1246,"width":363,"height":175},
		{"id":"3e1add3ff538d0c5","type":"text","text":"Pages with activity options are:\n\n14, ","x":837,"y":1307,"width":343,"height":133},
		{"id":"9e5d26b4245bb145","type":"text","text":"==Blackcoat pillars==","x":675,"y":869,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"83b988f7693e2932","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Blackcoats:\n> \n> What are Blackcoats?","x":1081,"y":-92,"width":301,"height":147},
		{"id":"f7cab5057ecb50db","type":"text","text":"Stage 4 collaborative worms to our cause in the name of ecosystem balance is something that The Corporation used, yet was not dependable as the formation of Stage 4 concept are not something repeatable under any known mean, yet operatives by themselves still tried to reach such highs in the name of our mission.\n\nSo a reward was due for those who refined their own skills and combat efficiency to the point of absolute perfection.","x":1490,"y":197,"width":598,"height":263},
		{"id":"edd28f4f36634d98","type":"text","text":"*Representatives and the \"High Rank\" position operatives across our history*: A brief glance ","x":1081,"y":246,"width":283,"height":165},
		{"id":"a3298f5fd1802975","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> To describe a Virtue is easy. They are individuals defined by their impossible nature. Each Virtue is effectively an anomaly. Hors catégorie. An out of bounds error. CAIN cannot explain them, it can only use them. There is nothing that implies a snotty, ashfaced, underfed CAT I Exorcist could, through perseverance or whatever else, ever become a Virtue.","x":1490,"y":-154,"width":455,"height":271},
		{"id":"34751eb525fa223c","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> The position of Virtue is, therefore, not useful as a motivator.","x":2240,"y":-92,"width":400,"height":147},
		{"id":"419fbc148dbb6ac3","type":"text","text":"Benefits where planned, counter-response protocols established ahead of time in case of something going wrong once the program was approved by The Administration and The Administrator itself at the time give it the green light.","x":2267,"y":250,"width":373,"height":157},
		{"id":"8064a6c904427cfc","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Therefore, it was the intention of Authority to create a position within CAIN that could fulfill this purpose. A rank that could be reached by climbing the rungs of CAIN’s ladder, which could earn an Exorcist respect.","x":2860,"y":-102,"width":564,"height":167},
		{"id":"9e6e5058fb8fede1","type":"text","text":"The program was initially known officially as the *\"Promotion program for those that Represent above the many\"* or sometime later was known between operatives and personnel: *Representatives* \n\n","x":2912,"y":266,"width":460,"height":125},
		{"id":"747a5676967ee983","type":"text","text":"Those who keep up good operative records and constant performances were then rewarded with more autonomy, resources and even higher access to information that the majority of humanity was not even aware that existed in the first place.\n\nSome cases allowed the \"authorization\" of formal relationships between operatives and representatives, or permits to circumvent certain rules or protocols as long as the teams working under the name of such representatives keep returning with more victories and less losses ","x":3580,"y":208,"width":612,"height":241},
		{"id":"f55479f434a67964","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Exorcists that became Blackcoats would be *loyal*, because they would be *thankful* that they were ever able to fall into such good graces with their hegemons.","x":3630,"y":-88,"width":512,"height":140},
		{"id":"101d59a662dea471","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> At least, that was the idea.","x":4420,"y":-73,"width":327,"height":111},
		{"id":"7e01d88b1070465d","type":"text","text":"But something was forming under The Administration gaze unnoticed, something that preceded The Great War itself","x":4394,"y":276,"width":380,"height":106},
		{"id":"a82f26d4cbec0b30","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> *~~And you shall know them by their billowing black cloaks, in dark mantles they will come, dark as the shadow of death, and under theIR WINGS THEY will rally the weak and the damned.~~*\n> \n> *:: CASTLE LAVATORY GRAFFITI*","x":4940,"y":-132,"width":500,"height":229},
		{"id":"787967490442db80","type":"text","text":"***THIS WORLD IS ROTTEN, FROM ITS VERY CORE TO THE SURFACE, SALVATION COME WITH REVOLUTION, WE REBEL NOT FOR OURSELVES BUT AGAINST THOSE ABOVE US, THE NARRATIVE MUST DIE SO WE CAN FINALLY REST***\n\n-Recorded speech from *REDACTED* to soon to be considered Exiled operatives","x":4959,"y":218,"width":463,"height":222},
		{"id":"35cc8ffe89595618","type":"text","text":"==WINGS OF HUBRIS==","x":6160,"y":329,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"b4298c728f427365","type":"text","text":"*The ashes preceding the war to end all wars*","x":5660,"y":329,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"1d710656e9fc81da","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> In practice, Blackcoats turned out to be a whole deal of trouble.","x":6127,"y":500,"width":317,"height":144},
		{"id":"57bd775f5faa408a","type":"text","text":"In truth, the cracks on the walls were always there and yet the program was just the final push to collapse everything on itself","x":5636,"y":517,"width":299,"height":127},
		{"id":"a67a6126cb51fbff","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> The rank, and the privileges that came with it, seeded nothing but arrogance and flamboyant disregard for CASTLE’s rules and commands. Many of these individuals would actively recruit lower Category Exorcists to their cliques and gangs, becoming ringleaders who completely disrupted the organisation and programming of Exorcist psychosystematics.","x":6107,"y":820,"width":357,"height":312},
		{"id":"9bb2b85332bfd876","type":"text","text":"Rampant ideals and inter-factions alongside constant conflicts of interest between what *The Administration* saw as The Cause and what *The Representatives* seen it has their own goal to reach by any means necessary.\n\nHierarchy established since millennia slowly corroding and burning down, reaching one day its very base. ","x":5617,"y":780,"width":338,"height":262},
		{"id":"2fd76ef730656b3f","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> After six years of the initial Blackcoat program, this type of behaviour came to a peak. CASTLE was all but waging war against the army of human weapons it was supposed to manage, suffering revenge after bloody revenge for their totalitarian treatment of Exorcists.","x":6107,"y":1240,"width":361,"height":285},
		{"id":"885fd66108cc8e42","type":"text","text":"Then suddenly as expected the table was flipped, nations and governments burning and collapsing in front of the very eyes of The Administration, something never seen before by even the old generation worms still around in those times:\n\n*A global scale conflict for no reason other than extinction for all parties involved*","x":5594,"y":1263,"width":384,"height":237},
		{"id":"ccf326522fb091fc","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> So, AUTHORITY intervened.","x":6136,"y":1660,"width":303,"height":96},
		{"id":"07dbbac9ffb20f2c","type":"text","text":"So, The Administration had to do something to recover control of the situation once again before it was too late.","x":5629,"y":1643,"width":315,"height":113},
		{"id":"352603d1f05268b0","type":"text","text":"==Burning of the crows==","x":5280,"y":2100,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"0c8afb5f3cf93f63","type":"text","text":"==Dust to dust==","x":4900,"y":2100,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"281cc6c5d0725c1f","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> In order to return total control over its Exorcists to the hands of CAIN’s branches, a total ==62% of all Exorcists ranked CAT4 and higher were purged== from the ranks of CAIN by the Virtues Justice, ████████, Faith, Charity and Prudence. Hope and Fortitude disobeyed this order. Hope was executed and replaced. Fortitude could not be meaningfully punished, only returned to their icy prison on SERAPH.","x":4320,"y":1986,"width":440,"height":289},
		{"id":"dc248f19787625ba","type":"text","text":"The after effects of The Great War","x":5280,"y":1810,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"39446d66cd6a6630","type":"text","text":"Control once again on the hands of humanity","x":4900,"y":1810,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"6f653bf9b3f6023e","type":"text","text":"The details during the initial formation of The Corporation and its response against rogue agents is vague has a consequence of prioritizing the emergency itself over record keeping, but in accordance to veterans and survivors The Administration had a total loss of at least its 72% of operatives across the board, including war casualties and Exiled operatives.","x":4290,"y":1757,"width":500,"height":166},
		{"id":"bde4a199a69bab70","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> What remained after those two days of blood and decimation was a severely pruned cadre of Blackcoats. Only those who swore ultimate loyalty, and those who could conceive of no life but one in CAIN’s service, remained.","x":3740,"y":2031,"width":420,"height":200},
		{"id":"04ec8a5379029f3f","type":"text","text":"What was left where a handful of original Representatives, or those who only during the war itself earned the right of promotion, in the old program.\n\nAnother security measure was established after the end of the conflict during the Purge Operation in the 1950s: Only Division oficially approved by The Administration can have representatives, High Rank operatives can't form their own factions anymore without proper clearance.","x":3710,"y":1716,"width":480,"height":249},
		{"id":"c77a9b61ee1c4382","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> These sects of loyalists and dogmatic characters now wear the crown among Exorcists. Though different in temperament, they all have one thing in common: without a doubt, they belong to CAIN.","x":3100,"y":2043,"width":441,"height":176},
		{"id":"f77dbd1fa0baffa5","type":"text","text":"Since then, only Division can have one High Rank Representative at a time to make sure The Administration personnel can keep control of operatives with higher amounts of resources to try anything drastic again.\n\nFor now, to be a Representative is to become not only a single flag showing to the world the absolute resolve of the operative in question but a greater responsibility that can be challenged and taken down if someone more capable or with a better record history of proficiency tries to take it by themselves.\n\nThe war shall not repeat ever again.","x":3044,"y":1695,"width":553,"height":291},
		{"id":"7fae56bc0b48f12b","type":"text","text":"==Blackcoat pillars==","x":2540,"y":1811,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"8921dd745f4883ff","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> In the following pages, you’ll find a way to add an ==extra layer of challenge and narrative== to CAIN by introducing loyalist gangs among Exorcists, led by notorious top-level Blackcoats, who will make your life harder and force you to navigate a landscape of tenuous alliances.","x":2463,"y":1400,"width":404,"height":240},
		{"id":"050f69e2cfbe6a94","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> These Blackcoat Pillars are regularly on the move, with their masses of loyal Exorcists in tow. Wherever they go, they’ll put their boot down and enforce CAIN’s iron will through their ==ruthless reign==. To avoid being crushed, you’ll have to invest in ==Loyalty== or play the dangerous game of currying ==Favours== from secretive benefactors.","x":2438,"y":994,"width":454,"height":246},
		{"id":"da1ab1447ba0c61f","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> How will you choose to live your life? Will you, too, grease the wheels of CAIN’s chariots, hoping to find some semblance of acceptance between their thundering spokes?","x":2493,"y":600,"width":345,"height":199},
		{"id":"a6e10396510e9853","type":"text","text":"==Pillar Overview:==\n\n(Check it if interested! personalized ones will be presented once Chapter 2 is reached :3)","x":3142,"y":623,"width":378,"height":153},
		{"id":"631ab1fe2ca7c9cd","type":"text","text":"==THE SHADOW OF WINGS==","x":3761,"y":670,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"72e81b8c1bcee239","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Whether you start your campaign using the Blackcoat Pillars, or whether you introduce them partway through the campaign, you’ll have to decide how characters have thus far related to their existence.","x":3708,"y":971,"width":357,"height":209},
		{"id":"7879ba38596f40c1","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Here are a few possible options, which include ==starting Loyalty== once the Pillars are in play.","x":3722,"y":1300,"width":330,"height":140},
		{"id":"d557d68c16ffa743","type":"text","text":"==Matching set==","x":3240,"y":1340,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"bfbbc5668f6d50e6","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> The reason you all know each other is that your service to CAIN so far has been under one particular master. Either through the connections of your Handler, the Exorcists who recruited you to CAIN, or some other circumstance, you’re already aligned with a Blackcoat Pillar.","x":3153,"y":948,"width":425,"height":255},
		{"id":"e0dc97f0ae69dcb6","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Have any of you ever seen your Pillar in person? How did their reign colour your experience in CAIN so far?","x":2926,"y":1440,"width":348,"height":170},
		{"id":"c41c386eee7a3498","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> If you choose this for Exorcists who are higher than CAT1; how come you’re only now finding out whose bidding you’ve been doing?","x":3365,"y":1480,"width":399,"height":183},
		{"id":"ce7b98791443ebe9","type":"text","text":"==Exorcists each start with one Loyalty token, of the same Pillar.==","x":4029,"y":1520,"width":323,"height":103},
		{"id":"613117d96bc27b96","type":"text","text":"==Structure Shift==","x":4540,"y":1370,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"54bec460b5e6dd78","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Each of you have a history, however brief, that doesn’t involve all the others. There were things you did that came before, that you kept from your team, or that they simply didn’t need to know.","x":4970,"y":1329,"width":361,"height":223},
		{"id":"6345a1e4c33b3052","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> How do each of you view the Pillar you served? And the other ones? Did you choose to serve, or were you forced?","x":5100,"y":971,"width":361,"height":142},
		{"id":"84c223cfb67e220d","type":"text","text":"==Exorcists each start with one Loyalty token, of any Pillar.==","x":5127,"y":780,"width":307,"height":116},
		{"id":"e5d3b7f5032a4dae","type":"text","text":"==Cloudburst==","x":4352,"y":1180,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"d7fbe1c3136f4b63","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> The world of Blackcoats was mostly an uneasy formless darkness, passing over the you like clouds. You didn’t have to deal with the movements of giants, so you didn’t. Heavy names, you found, were better left unweighed.","x":4304,"y":826,"width":346,"height":244},
		{"id":"de6a01c00aea0187","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> But trouble has found you at last. With the arrival of a Pillar and their loyalists at the facility where you reside, you can no longer avoid their game.","x":4293,"y":565,"width":368,"height":165},
		{"id":"5a6e9e887d4a543c","type":"text","text":"==What will you do now?==","x":4790,"y":614,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"e9a35935fbab047f","type":"text","text":"==Exorcists start with no Loyalty tokens. This will be more difficult.==","x":4762,"y":838,"width":307,"height":90},
		{"id":"4ea5bda9292f6446","type":"text","text":"==Reigning pillars==","x":2160,"y":1811,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"81e9183c82697d03","type":"text","text":"*IT'S THEIRS*","x":1639,"y":2079,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"b3e61db88ae34ca4","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Once the Blackcoat Pillars are in play, time between hunts will always be defined by an ==reigning Pillar==. This means that for one reason or the other, said Pillar defines the atmosphere in the CAIN facility where the Exorcists reside, even if it’s only temporarily. This could mean:","x":1557,"y":1720,"width":415,"height":243},
		{"id":"45caff3ef78a2ce7","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> The facility already contains a large number of Exorcists loyal to this Pillar, and they operate closely with CASTLE staff, perhaps having a connection to the facility’s CASTLE chief. \n> \n> If this is true…","x":1573,"y":2280,"width":382,"height":226},
		{"id":"f92e9317042cfbf3","type":"text","text":"==• … why is this facility important to them?==","x":1160,"y":2364,"width":259,"height":58},
		{"id":"c2bafd12583c0ec9","type":"text","text":"==• … how do they work together with CASTLE?==","x":1639,"y":2643,"width":250,"height":77},
		{"id":"433a5ba371dd3db3","type":"text","text":"==• … what marks their signature touch?==","x":2159,"y":2364,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"619cabd278c8d83d","type":"text","text":"\"Why is the Representative around the nation physically at the present time?\"","x":1159,"y":2840,"width":260,"height":88},
		{"id":"7f333622ec98d299","type":"text","text":"(Reigning pillars can be seen working as generally *The Guy* pulling up work around the area or the nation, and you have to deal with them A. Physically B. Administrative and C, politically)\n\n(You can see it as less of a \"I am here and I am trouble\" and more of \"So this is the guy I have to deal with to reach my message to logistics\" kind of treatment...although it can get *bad* if you try to act as if you are better than them for sure) ","x":1090,"y":1944,"width":400,"height":271},
		{"id":"f3a3e01fd604ebbe","type":"text","text":"\"What is their own way of dealing with logistics between you and Administration?\"","x":1624,"y":2840,"width":281,"height":90},
		{"id":"179d11d0dc406975","type":"text","text":"What is their distinctive characteristic or way of working that marks their presence? ","x":2126,"y":2840,"width":316,"height":101},
		{"id":"227264f8dc175540","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> The Pillar is visiting the facility and is accompanied by a large number of their loyal Exorcists. The *local* CASTLE staff may dislike them, but they understand that the Pillar is here on the order of higher administration, perhaps even AUTHORITY themselves. \n> \n> If this is true…","x":1522,"y":3220,"width":486,"height":240},
		{"id":"81f04a62e91c70be","type":"text","text":"==• … why are they here? Was their visit announced?==","x":1031,"y":3304,"width":258,"height":72},
		{"id":"4316c9b6b3ffcace","type":"text","text":"==• … does the facility’s administration like to see them coming?==","x":1633,"y":3640,"width":264,"height":96},
		{"id":"296275164ad3aaa1","type":"text","text":"==• … Is the official reason for their visit the *real* reason?==","x":2260,"y":3304,"width":267,"height":73},
		{"id":"34620ad0acd0ce15","type":"text","text":"\"Is the Division present in the name of its function or by the orders of the Representative?\"","x":1016,"y":3840,"width":288,"height":92},
		{"id":"3805be2773ffc7f8","type":"text","text":"\"Is the Nation or locally established personnel in line with the representative? or sees them as trouble regardless of true intent?\"","x":1605,"y":3840,"width":321,"height":125},
		{"id":"ff74bfc3db10d717","type":"text","text":"\"...is the representative *actually* saying the truth about their reasons why they are interfering in regular workflow?\"","x":2248,"y":3856,"width":291,"height":109},
		{"id":"252cdaa6f37326f8","type":"text","text":"CHOOSING A PILLAR","x":1633,"y":4280,"width":250,"height":50},
		{"id":"2f7ec08d55173b87","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> At the end of every hunt, a random person is chosen from the players ==and== the Admin. \n> \n> That person chooses which Pillar will reign until the end of the next hunt.","x":1583,"y":4460,"width":351,"height":218},
		{"id":"2cf4c0d6471da187","type":"text","text":"==LOYALTY==","x":2665,"y":2364,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"a99bdd5ef765db8f","type":"text","text":"==Loyalty tokens==","x":2665,"y":2620,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"aa25987d768fb408","type":"text","text":"==Loyalty tokens are distinct per Pillar.==","x":2317,"y":3100,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"e6f1a9d6912f26d4","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Exorcists can possess ==any mix== of tokens. Pursuing mixed loyalties means you’ll be on shakier grounds when your own Loyalty is tested, but it may make you a useful “fixer” for your group, being able to spend tokens in many different scenarios.","x":2612,"y":3182,"width":379,"height":245},
		{"id":"a1d0cb87384ad2c0","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Each Exorcist has the capacity to gather ==Loyalty tokens== by completing objectives during hunts.","x":2624,"y":2823,"width":356,"height":135},
		{"id":"c3705b453cdd50c8","type":"text","text":"Here are two example Exorcists with some Loyalty tokens:","x":2466,"y":3620,"width":293,"height":60},
		{"id":"07951a644c4f0863","type":"file","file":"Cain-Glory unto them-summary/Pasted image 20261022222455.png","x":2559,"y":3800,"width":400,"height":301},
		{"id":"1569bf4e38b1a18c","type":"text","text":"==THE TEST OF LOYALTY==","x":3180,"y":3275,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"3d07762c7da4b78b","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> • How does the Pillar’s presence (personal or through their underlings) sweep through the facility?","x":3551,"y":2958,"width":385,"height":143},
		{"id":"075f3331da4bdc89","type":"text","text":"\"How is the presence or direct interference of the Representative or its followers affects the team directly or not?\"","x":3060,"y":2969,"width":343,"height":121},
		{"id":"6294d8d33f06d4c4","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Like with the choice for the reigning pillar, a ==random person== is chosen to be the subject of this Loyalty test, from the players and the Admin. If the Admin is chosen, they decide which player’s Loyalty is tested that time.","x":4180,"y":3213,"width":428,"height":184},
		{"id":"6af2609fd215ef31","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> That person rolls a d6, and ==adds their number of Loyalty tokens for that Pillar to the result==. This does not consume the tokens.","x":4800,"y":3224,"width":397,"height":163},
		{"id":"d098860f68a51b44","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Other players can also add Loyalty tokens of that Pillar to this roll, but this ==does== consume those tokens as they push their luck to save the tested player’s skin.","x":4815,"y":2860,"width":368,"height":195},
		{"id":"620c67db7eb2c090","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Any player may also ==spend 2 tokens of a different Pillar== to raise the result by 1. When doing so, said Pillar or one of their lieutenants intervenes on your behalf","x":4796,"y":3540,"width":406,"height":164},
		{"id":"b59026db0105cbc6","type":"text","text":"If the results are:","x":5540,"y":3276,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"7870f637c3932566","type":"text","text":"==FINAL RESULT 6(+)==","x":5319,"y":3042,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"07a56e8f011e01da","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Your loyalty goes unquestioned. You’re in the reigning Pillar’s good graces, and their presence is markedly relaxed… for now.","x":5292,"y":2620,"width":305,"height":187},
		{"id":"684fa756c685797c","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> *The reigning Pillar’s Loyalty tokens can be exchanged for items from their prestige section until the next hunt.*","x":5262,"y":2320,"width":364,"height":175},
		{"id":"13796ab93d22b418","type":"text","text":"• What did you do that made their day?","x":4815,"y":2377,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"7f429fe54789ac03","type":"text","text":"==FINAL RESULT 5 or 4==","x":5840,"y":3042,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"82f69aa7d5201ce3","type":"text","text":"• How are less fortunate Exorcists treated while you enjoy privilege?","x":4776,"y":2520,"width":289,"height":67},
		{"id":"eb6c868de0fb3fb6","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> The Pillar and their loyalists aren’t out to mess with you, but they certainly don’t care to please. Going about your business is more difficult than it usually is, unless you can find ways around the Pillar’s ruthless command.","x":5775,"y":2593,"width":380,"height":214},
		{"id":"de7cfb6730c4eac5","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> *To buy kit expansions or pursue an activity before the next hunt, you must spend a Loyalty token.*","x":5784,"y":2320,"width":362,"height":161},
		{"id":"5543b4d16ace0bea","type":"text","text":"• How are the Pillar and their loyalists pushing the facility past its usual capacity? What mandate do they have to do so?","x":6352,"y":2186,"width":328,"height":134},
		{"id":"ec94d934e843bcff","type":"text","text":"• If you spend a Loyalty token to access kit expansions and activities, and the token is of a different Pillar than the reigning one, how is their influence benefiting you even now?","x":6363,"y":2544,"width":317,"height":156},
		{"id":"567876d1bdf3a900","type":"text","text":"\"How is the presence of a complete division or higher than expected workload in a single logistics line is affecting its functions? what are they enforcing in the process?\"","x":6880,"y":2179,"width":356,"height":148},
		{"id":"018c0bb656509957","type":"text","text":"• How is CASTLE, begrudgingly or willingly, abetting them?","x":6366,"y":2401,"width":301,"height":69},
		{"id":"f43632f165becee5","type":"text","text":"\"Why is the local authorities or even The Administration hand is being held back for interfering or why is letting it happen willingly?\"","x":6899,"y":2413,"width":318,"height":115},
		{"id":"051d7218c92743b0","type":"text","text":"\"How is using another division influence in your benefit to access priority in equipment acquisition and allowed activities from The Administration catalogue? how is this pulling of strings benefiting you directly going forwards?\"","x":6902,"y":2641,"width":334,"height":166},
		{"id":"0a5c2d9334d54818","type":"text","text":"==FINAL RESULT 3 or 2==","x":5319,"y":3540,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"81a8329d2b6d3df1","type":"text","text":"==FINAL RESULT 1==","x":5840,"y":3540,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"6251f2fa64abaf2e","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> You’re not in good books with the Pillar, and they’re going to make it very clear what happens to any Exorcists who think they can afford to act out on their watch.","x":5243,"y":3680,"width":403,"height":173},
		{"id":"15f01f9518082f16","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> 2. A squad of the Pillar’s Exorcists will “help” you on the next hunt. They retreat to “look for the Palace” once you’ve suffered an Injury, and will create dangerous situations on purpose (or hurt you personally if they catch you alone)","x":4774,"y":4436,"width":426,"height":222},
		{"id":"fa9846f4ebb4b7c3","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> 1. You start the next hunt with 1 stress, or 3 if you had any Injuries last hunt. How were you kept from recovering?","x":4774,"y":4200,"width":395,"height":138},
		{"id":"0194e31fb56126bb","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> *The Pillar activates their Show of Force until the end of the next hunt.*","x":5260,"y":3960,"width":370,"height":120},
		{"id":"a9b839aca690caeb","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> *Additionally, each Exorcist chooses one:*","x":5296,"y":4214,"width":299,"height":110},
		{"id":"117629b806c994a6","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> 3. Choose two skills you have at least one rank in. Those skills have 1 fewer rank next hunt. How were you thrown off your game?","x":5727,"y":4176,"width":363,"height":186},
		{"id":"267ad94b9e0c6914","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> 4. You start the next hunt with erratic Psyche Bursts that have a 1 in 2 chance to inflict 1 Sin if used. Bursts gained during the hunt are normal. How was your mind disturbed?","x":5727,"y":4449,"width":377,"height":196},
		{"id":"095a410542779fda","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> You’ve made yourself an active problem for the Pillar - a thorn in their eye. They are in the worst possible mood, and arrange to have you done away with. What did you do?","x":5765,"y":3720,"width":400,"height":163},
		{"id":"ec8d745031edf538","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> The Pillar activates their Show of Force until the end of the next hunt.","x":6200,"y":3971,"width":402,"height":109},
		{"id":"5a5e2c5aa477538a","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Next hunt, they send a Death Squad after you.","x":6780,"y":3983,"width":418,"height":86},
		{"id":"b2223f16d988ef66","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> The types of enemies sent include all enemies of CAT and below listed on that Pillar’s entry","x":6812,"y":3700,"width":355,"height":150},
		{"id":"0ecd40f711d2b967","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> If the Pillar themself is part of the Death Squad, they retreat from the hunt once their execution talisman has been fully slashed. Should they disgrace themselves this way, the next hunt will be their ==Fatal Decree==.","x":7420,"y":3927,"width":400,"height":197},
		{"id":"e7b012113729998e","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Read more about Fatal Decrees on page ==93.==","x":6824,"y":4220,"width":332,"height":112},
		{"id":"b117e0a2ca563167","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> As soon as it’s clear which Blackcoat Pillar dominates the playing field, this reigning Pillar will ==test the Loyalty of an Exorcist, and therefore by proxy, the group.==","x":3524,"y":3211,"width":440,"height":186},
		{"id":"1933b8fe9d995373","type":"text","text":"==LOYALTY OBJECTIVES==","x":3278,"y":3590,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"e9b4ff1857d74285","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> In order to ==gain Loyalty tokens== from a Pillar, you must further their personal goals within CAIN and prove that you’re a worthy asset, that you jump when they say “jump,” and that you kill when they say “kill.”","x":3700,"y":3524,"width":400,"height":192},
		{"id":"cee96b2f3ca656f8","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Every time you start a hunt, while you are briefed on its basic information, you are also offered the chance to take on a ==Loyalty objective== for a Pillar.","x":3727,"y":3900,"width":347,"height":200},
		{"id":"b0305a23fa2d4a2c","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> A Loyalty objective is a side goal to complete during the Hunt. They range from undertaking specific actions, to upholding a vow, to taking on added difficulty effects.","x":3720,"y":4280,"width":362,"height":195},
		{"id":"3e2aca78d465d742","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> When choosing a Loyalty objective, players can pick from:","x":3720,"y":4620,"width":362,"height":121},
		{"id":"642abe9f98db39bb","type":"text","text":"(Page 24!)","x":3140,"y":4475,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"4012c6da5bb72432","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> • The objectives on a Pillar’s own page.","x":3748,"y":4960,"width":306,"height":106},
		{"id":"ee84cff7f9fac092","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> • The three basic objectives below that can be used for any Pillar","x":3080,"y":4624,"width":370,"height":113},
		{"id":"1934ad11628bbbcc","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> • Any additional objectives your Admin makes available.","x":4280,"y":4627,"width":314,"height":108},
		{"id":"291529b6f4e32798","type":"text","text":"==SHOWS OF FORCE==","x":6480,"y":3216,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"f517e221b337a01b","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> When one does not have the luxury to leverage their standing with the reigning Pillar, they must face the flood of their influence head-on, and desperately struggle to float.","x":6990,"y":3153,"width":403,"height":187},
		{"id":"f228b59c6a753af2","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Whether punishment for the Exorcists’ actions, or simply a matter of tyranny born from their vicious hearts, a Pillar’s ==Show of Force== inflicts a dire situation between hunts, unique to each Pillar.","x":7680,"y":3154,"width":392,"height":186},
		{"id":"2e94992437940359","type":"text","text":"==DEATH SQUADS==","x":6374,"y":4376,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"63070b2d890f7e08","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> When a Pillar sends a Death Squad after you, they’re taking their chance to have you killed in a situation where the deaths of unruly Exorcists can easily be ascribed to a malevolent Sin or the chaos of a hunt.","x":6312,"y":4645,"width":374,"height":215},
		{"id":"ff99b4500feca66d","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> The level of danger created by such a reckoning depends on your Exorcists’ CAT. Each Pillar sends different enemies after you depending on the height of your CAT, only stepping in themselves at CAT 5.","x":6326,"y":5040,"width":347,"height":227},
		{"id":"1a37ac2fd8f88d49","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Each level ==expands upon== the previous levels in the following ways:","x":6336,"y":5520,"width":328,"height":143},
		{"id":"d1740b967c169ee8","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==• Who== does the Pillar send? Do they send underling Exorcists to do their bidding, do they also employ a ==special enemy type==, or do they even step in themselves along with these followers?","x":5780,"y":5501,"width":382,"height":181},
		{"id":"4aca8da8202c4c70","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Which ==Tension Moves== can the Death Squad employ? A number of times per hunt equal to your CAT, when the tension talisman fills, the Death Squad can make a Tension Move on top of the one that the Sin makes.","x":6317,"y":5780,"width":366,"height":228},
		{"id":"b6822c9c864c79fe","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Enemies sent by a Pillar ==can be executed== when their talisman is fully slashed. If you do so, however, your group loses ==any remaining Loyalty tokens== they had for the Pillar who sent them.","x":6940,"y":5513,"width":510,"height":158},
		{"id":"2e97d19a0fa7c32a","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Alternatively, they can be let off with an injury and a warning. This way, tokens are not impacted. Either way, they leave the hunt","x":7760,"y":5502,"width":375,"height":181},
		{"id":"c6adac6acf99e236","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> When a Death Squad is deployed, answer the following:","x":8440,"y":5535,"width":364,"height":114},
		{"id":"d770e04cb15b58fa","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> • Are the Exorcists aware the Death Squad is also deploying to their hunt?","x":8000,"y":5180,"width":457,"height":145},
		{"id":"e7f5434e3c5e1224","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> • If they are, under what guise are they doing so? Are they supposed to be handling a different part of the investigation area? A backup squad?","x":8615,"y":5157,"width":385,"height":168},
		{"id":"786fc2619c587b8e","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> • What excuse will they put forward to justify their actions after the hunt? How will their Pillar cover for them?","x":8076,"y":5880,"width":306,"height":157},
		{"id":"56ea19fa5f4723b9","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> • Do the Exorcists know any of them? Is there an NPC Exorcist keen to be a part of the Death Squad?","x":8634,"y":5888,"width":347,"height":142},
		{"id":"ada4ac37f8e81839","type":"file","file":"Cain-Glory unto them-summary/Pasted image 20261023150601.png","x":6860,"y":5064,"width":399,"height":180},
		{"id":"2e29d0725d7b74b7","type":"text","text":"==COMBINING ENEMIES==","x":9179,"y":5561,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"d6bd76b8a885d5c8","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> In situations where the Sin’s assault coincides with the presence of enemy Exorcists, it’s possible that they also become targets of the Sin or its Traces","x":9640,"y":5510,"width":397,"height":162},
		{"id":"7498fbdd2921f677","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> In such cases, the enemy Exorcists act like (additional) Traces, hoping to throw the players into the Sin’s claws or into a horde of smaller foes.","x":9665,"y":5820,"width":347,"height":164},
		{"id":"1b5b3a716744f363","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> However, when a 6 is rolled on a risk die, attacks from the Sin or its Traces slash the enemy Exorcists’ talisman once and force them to back out of the scene (or execute them if that fully slashes the talisman).","x":9640,"y":5200,"width":400,"height":183},
		{"id":"f558652d1920c18b","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Enemy Exorcists also act like Traces in a conflict with a ==Blackcoat Pillar.==","x":10220,"y":5536,"width":348,"height":110},
		{"id":"604ba7eabd6b52f7","type":"text","text":"==THE FINAL CONFLICT==","x":9220,"y":5929,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"aa8f97adc3613a6c","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> When enemy Exorcists are present during a hunt, and haven’t been defeated before the final conflict against the Sin arrives, they’ll use one of these tactics:","x":9157,"y":6200,"width":376,"height":175},
		{"id":"c1c3dc9165e8ab4c","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> • If they’re cocky, make them attack the players right ==before== they enter the Sin’s palace. They assume they can finish the job themselves after dealing with the players.","x":8520,"y":6211,"width":496,"height":154},
		{"id":"6b0fdd1a8a4b2983","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> • If they love chaos and danger, ==add== them to the Sin fight, making them function as above, but not having the option to retreat. They take a slash from Sin attacks when a 6 is rolled on a risk die, once per round.","x":9145,"y":6520,"width":401,"height":185},
		{"id":"623bb7f2f9ba4173","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> • If they’re cowardly or scheming, make them attack the players right ==after== they’ve executed the Sin. They’ve bided their time to strike until the short window before extraction.","x":9680,"y":6206,"width":412,"height":164},
		{"id":"6521898b3ecdade7","type":"text","text":"==Exorcists talisman==","x":9803,"y":6496,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"7b0fe1969fbed141","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==EXORCISTS==\n> \n> It’s a given of CAIN dogma that Exorcists are not human, and that if they are disobedient and unruly, their death is as natural as the killing of a pest. So, as soon as there is some prerogative to do so, Exorcists full of hatred for their own kind will rapidly turn on their peers — if anything to punish them for grasping at a kind of freedom and personhood they themselves have long since given up on.\n> \n> ==Exorcists:== Execution talisman 2 (weak duo), 4 (weak group), 6 (experienced group). They have the following features:\n> \n> • Full of stress and self-loathing. Actions that take advantage of this roll +1D against them.\n> \n> • When talisman is fully slashed, roll 1d3: (1) Extend talisman by one slash. This can only trigger once.\n> \n> • Provide 1 (execution talisman 1-5) or 1d3 (execution talisman 6+) scrip and XP if you return their bodies to CAIN post-humously.\n> \n> ==Reactions:==\n> \n> ==Inflict Stress.== Attacks with: service weapons, psychic blasts, destructive techniques. ==(1)==: 4 stress, ==(2-3):== 3 stress, ==(4+):== 2 stress\n> \n> ==Or:== create a complication or threat: Collapse part of a building, unleash a psychic torrent, employ a reality-warping technique, reveal a horrifying sin mark, lose their mind.","x":9516,"y":6780,"width":825,"height":591},
		{"id":"72b8d5da97b411f4","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> In the case of a ==Blackcoat Pillar==, whom the players will always have to face in a final conflict scene, you can:","x":8200,"y":6473,"width":364,"height":140},
		{"id":"ced1d8401a3bf70f","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> • Make the Pillar attack the players right ==before== they enter the Sin’s palace, sure they can deal with the Sin themselves afterwards (This might cause Pressure to increase as the confrontation with the Sin is stalled).","x":7880,"y":6194,"width":399,"height":188},
		{"id":"77bc9e6c2eae8983","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==• Replace== the Sin fight with a Pillar fight: the Pillar has freshly executed the Sin as the players arrive, and will now do what they came here to do. (This is best foreshadowed to manage player expectations).","x":7520,"y":6460,"width":459,"height":166},
		{"id":"d49c694de70e6a63","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Make the Pillar attack the players ==after== they’ve executed the Sin, taking no chances. (This very mean)","x":8202,"y":6740,"width":361,"height":137},
		{"id":"141b996b6f35ff43","type":"text","text":"==FAVOURS== ","x":2345,"y":4501,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"295165847d15d8d7","type":"text","text":"The Administration always keep its gaze to those in Higher Ranks, and it is the responsibility of the Representatives to keep their eye and hears for those against our cause directly or indirectly, but there is always a rogue agent of a operative who plays on both sides at the same time away of every radar and security check","x":2523,"y":4799,"width":357,"height":227},
		{"id":"05692609b9381d2a","type":"text","text":"The Great War and its direct consequences can be considered now as old human history thanks to the active effort of The Corporation in damage control not only in its official closure but the years to come after 1950s.\n\nEven then, young operatives and those with bad intentions are a volatile mix bound to go wrong if left to grow or supported by those who only want a \"benefit\" or a \"profit\" out of it","x":2480,"y":5134,"width":444,"height":252},
		{"id":"cd423720244b88f1","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Despite the iron fist with which the Blackcoat Pillars keep CAIN’s rank and file under their (and ultimately, AUTHORITY’s) thumb, not all CAIN members see this as the way things ought to be.","x":2100,"y":4799,"width":340,"height":221},
		{"id":"bea52e8ed381863f","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> For some cunning young Exorcists, the Burning of Crows is merely history, a past tense implying that disobedience simply requires some diplomatic finesse to fly.","x":2100,"y":5160,"width":340,"height":200},
		{"id":"84bfca7b0206d1b7","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> For some who are old enough to have witnessed the Burning, staff and Exorcists alike, it was a grievous, unwarranted overreach of AUTHORITY that they have not forgotten, nor forgiven.","x":2076,"y":5520,"width":388,"height":200},
		{"id":"a33d5a3c4b32928f","type":"text","text":"For those who participated or even lived through the war at any point of its execution, they were able to connects the dots easily and find someone to blame for such an incident to form and grow out of control exponentially: The Administration.\n\nIn truth, some had difficulties in completely forgiving those working above them, for such great loss in human lives and the global trust to worms slowly but surely being prepared for centuries before being violently shattered by dangerous, volatile and radical worms both working in secret under The Administration gaze and those outside their control","x":2480,"y":5520,"width":444,"height":320},
		{"id":"64e3dda9853948c9","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> If you are loathe to be the Blackcoat Pillars’ dog, you may be able to mitigate their scrutiny another way: through ==Favours==.","x":2111,"y":5980,"width":318,"height":160},
		{"id":"1f51e5f077b9adfb","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Favours can substitute Loyalty tokens in any of their uses, but they are ==also spent when used to contribute to one’s own Loyalty test==, as opposed to Loyalty tokens.","x":2120,"y":6420,"width":301,"height":220},
		{"id":"a2b428b1c42ee1f3","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> As these Favours primarily come from influential individuals or groups within CAIN who are willing to risk their neck to give you some leeway, you must also be careful not to ==expose your benefactors==. If caught, they may be violently reprimanded and your ability to gain Favours from them may collapse.","x":2580,"y":6780,"width":460,"height":225},
		{"id":"823abbe29e9fe335","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Sustaining yourself only on Favours rather than licking any boot is the mark of a true punk, but also quite difficult.","x":3300,"y":6823,"width":378,"height":139},
		{"id":"5ec74d00ff6d9248","type":"text","text":"==BENEFACTORS==","x":3364,"y":7120,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"b97c6257884f3404","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> There are three types of benefactors that you can gain Favour from: ==Jobbers==, ==Partisans== and ==Spies==.","x":3312,"y":7360,"width":354,"height":144},
		{"id":"aabf8e6507d582b3","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==• Partisans== are people who want CAIN to change, but are constrained by its tyrannical bureaucracy in the light. So, they have to move pieces in shadow. Examples of Partisans include idealistic branch managers or self-assured young Exorcists with a plan. Partisans undermine CAIN’s authority with their activity, but typically do not want to cause the organisation’s downfall.","x":3278,"y":7680,"width":422,"height":287},
		{"id":"82523d0390b52e39","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==• Spies==, on the other hand, are characterised by the fact that they actively work against CAIN’s interests. They can be individuals from within who have grown to hate the system, or even infiltrants from world governments or investigation agencies. These individuals obscure their true identity even from you, using codenames.","x":3940,"y":7324,"width":527,"height":216},
		{"id":"1cf7c04a9087f22e","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==• Jobbers== will cover for you for with a simple goal in mind: scrip. You pay them, they get someone off your back for a while. It’s a simple trade. These benefactors have the least vision, and are often low ranking Exorcists desperate for a better meal plan and willing to act up a little bit for cash.","x":2640,"y":7309,"width":400,"height":247},
		{"id":"99685206853c6555","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Each of these benefactors has something different that they want from you in exchange for Favours.","x":3338,"y":8120,"width":302,"height":200},
		{"id":"cf2a6c17897c43e0","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> • Jobbers want ==scrip.==","x":2940,"y":8580,"width":231,"height":87},
		{"id":"b7be8a6930ce585f","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> • Partisans want you to complete objectives ==between hunts==.","x":3325,"y":8580,"width":328,"height":107},
		{"id":"bf053655496aea73","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> • Spies want you to complete objectives ==during hunts.==","x":3740,"y":8582,"width":318,"height":105},
		{"id":"b75e7fbc84bbf54d","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> At ==any== point between hunts, you may choose a benefactor and roll on the appropriate table below to gain an objective (and mark slashes on their Heat talisman). There can only be one objective active for each Benefactor, and the player who rolled it must be the one to fulfill it (or part of the fulfilling group if multiple Exorcists are required).","x":3221,"y":8940,"width":536,"height":220},
		{"id":"220df8d42b383f04","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==Jobber objectives:==\n> \n> ==1-3==: pay 5 scrip. Gain a Favour.\n> \n> ==4-5==: pay 6 scrip. Gain a Favour\n> \n> ==6==: pay 7 scrip. Gain two Favours.","x":2860,"y":9340,"width":336,"height":211},
		{"id":"cbf97fe83213e37e","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==Partisan Objectives== \n> \n> ==1-3:== At least two Exorcists must carry out the Grunge Work activity before your next hunt. The player who rolled this objective gains a Favour.\n> \n> ==4-5==: You must successfully carry out the Muster activity before your next hunt. Gain a Favour\n> \n> ==6:== At least two Exorcists must successfully carry out the Wiretap activity before your next hunt. They each gain a Favour.","x":3273,"y":9320,"width":432,"height":331},
		{"id":"a6cf957a144d2910","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ==Spy objectives==\n> \n> ==1-3:== During the next hunt, meet an outside agent for an interview in a difficult to reach place designated by the Spy. Gain two Favours\n> \n> \n> ==4-5:== During the next hunt, drop off an Occult Item in a difficult to reach place designated by the Spy. Gain two Favours.\n> \n> ==6:== During the next hunt, find a human designated by the Spy and ==roll 1d2==. ==1==: execute them, ==2:== smuggle them into CAIN. Gain two Favours.","x":3780,"y":9320,"width":464,"height":351},
		{"id":"a129df4b8da967d1","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Favours are added to your pool of Loyalty tokens as soon as you complete the objective.","x":3322,"y":9820,"width":335,"height":140},
		{"id":"30a1e9ab33c0e8f3","type":"text","text":"\"If there is a goal, there is a way to reach it\" is a statement believed and repeated generally by those new enough in active service to still believe in the hope of a better day, returning back home or just staying alive until retirement.\n\nFor others, it means how to work around the displayed rules and bend them enough until you can get the benefits without playing from the hand of the Representatives who could, in some degree depending of who you worked for, care less if you die or live doing their objectives and tasks in active service.","x":2491,"y":5980,"width":423,"height":340},
		{"id":"7cc039a5831169e9","type":"text","text":"Muster/wiretap/smokescreen activities descriptions are present on page 49 for revision sake.","x":2540,"y":9731,"width":280,"height":89},
		{"id":"7ccbc35b34688750","type":"text","text":"==Heat talismans==","x":3008,"y":10134,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"07fa83b484ee939f","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Although you may roll any number of times to get more benefactor objectives, this isn’t without cost. Each time you roll for an objective with a particular benefactor, you slash their ==heat talisman== 1d3 times.","x":2955,"y":10400,"width":357,"height":218},
		{"id":"739a27bceabb63f0","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Jobbers can take a maximum of ==6== heat. Partisans can take ==5==. Spies can take ==3==.","x":2460,"y":10440,"width":313,"height":138},
		{"id":"8f451db26671793d","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> When a benefactor’s heat talisman is ==fully slashed==, they have to lay low. They’re no longer available to take objectives from until their heat is reduced.","x":3480,"y":10429,"width":396,"height":161},
		{"id":"cc8ddd0a0879a8b1","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> After each hunt, one slash is cleared from each benefactor’s heat talisman. The ==Smokescreen== activity may clear an additional slash from a chosen benefactor’s heat talisman.","x":2969,"y":10780,"width":330,"height":217},
		{"id":"740fbf1eba98f1cb","type":"text","text":"==Benefactors unmasked==","x":3490,"y":10134,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"97b4a1d8cf82c674","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> When a number of slashes ==exceed== a benefactor’s heat talisman, however, CAIN drags them out and brutally makes an example out of them. They are permanently removed from the list of benefactors, all currently standing objectives from all benefactors are removed, and all benefactors lay low (as though their heat gauge was filled).","x":3980,"y":10029,"width":425,"height":270},
		{"id":"4290fb561743f550","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> If a ==Spy== or ==Partisan== was outed this way, the player who caused their Heat to exceed the talisman loses 1d3 random tokens from their Loyalty and Favours.","x":3997,"y":9760,"width":391,"height":164},
		{"id":"b1cd54c68e733c69","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Depending on your game, you can decide how many benefactors of each type are available. Make sure the threat of losing benefactors stays real.","x":4620,"y":10076,"width":380,"height":177},
		{"id":"6ee6af09a342eb42","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Adding a new benefactor to the roster should typically be the result of story progression or one or more Exorcists putting in significant effort to find more connections that they can draw Favours from.","x":3994,"y":10429,"width":398,"height":199},
		{"id":"a933599f9d573f68","type":"text","text":"==FATAL DECREES== ","x":8100,"y":3996,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"d924016319632c35","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> When a Pillar is personally defeated by Exorcists during a Death Squad incursion, AUTHORITY itself assesses their loyalty to have been compromised by psychic contamination, and decides that they are no longer fit to function as top level agents of CAIN.","x":8580,"y":3914,"width":418,"height":225},
		{"id":"744c1959be7fc1d2","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> To go brazenly against protocol and attempt to destroy valuable CAT5 CAIN assets without clearance from AUTHORITY is to sign your own death warrant.","x":8620,"y":3560,"width":338,"height":200},
		{"id":"964f158b3df77fb3","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> When embarking on a Fatal Decree, you are effectively fulfilling the role that a Death Squad may have fulfilled for you - with the exception that this attack is sanctioned by AUTHORITY itself.","x":9660,"y":3935,"width":423,"height":183},
		{"id":"74692f895b6ce9b0","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> In theory, the Pillar will be deployed to a false mission area by their administrative contacts; an environment far from civilian sites, where it will be easy to catch them alone and hunt them down.","x":9702,"y":3580,"width":338,"height":220},
		{"id":"842ec182c10813a8","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> As such, a ==Fatal Decree== is issued: the Pillar is designated as a ==Hunt target== and the player Exorcists are assigned to carry out their execution immediately","x":9100,"y":3560,"width":338,"height":197},
		{"id":"dfaa959ef8913dbd","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> Unfortunately for you and CAIN, this goes according to plan in ==none== of the Fatal Decree scenarios in this book.","x":10180,"y":3619,"width":392,"height":142},
		{"id":"0fc1d4bfb4e72807","type":"text","text":"==PLAN AND PRACTICE==","x":9380,"y":3840,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"53949425cb421222","type":"text","text":"==CRISIS ACTION==","x":10680,"y":3840,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"54abff80a4983c51","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> The Fatal Decree scenarios occur when the previously mentioned trap has already failed. The Pillar has caught on and fled, or has set themselves up to oppose you, knowing of your approach.","x":10616,"y":4018,"width":378,"height":200},
		{"id":"53e69d31d2739bd3","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> As soon as you embark on this hunt - in some cases without the chance for any preparation - you’ll be faced with a Sin with unique Domains and unexpected tricks.","x":11180,"y":4030,"width":400,"height":177},
		{"id":"3f2be78564153b84","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> The foes you’ll look in the eye in these battles will be unlike what you normally face as an exorcist. They will reflect your future fate if you are ever too disloyal. And they will try to break that fate and slit your throat with its shards.","x":11175,"y":3653,"width":411,"height":216},
		{"id":"45257d26bbad2150","type":"text","text":"==PERFECT SINS==","x":11680,"y":3480,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"52be7a3f7da5cc9b","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> All the Sins you’ll face on Fatal Decree Hunts are ==Perfect Sins== as described in ==Games for Freaks #3==. This means that they have a number of extra abilities compared to normal Sins.","x":12097,"y":3411,"width":363,"height":199},
		{"id":"eeddd94c16033cbe","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> The Fatal Decree Sins, however, alter these abilities slightly, as follows:","x":12126,"y":3761,"width":306,"height":143},
		{"id":"800b425d51545023","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> • FD Hunt Sins only have three “Perfect” abilities.","x":11720,"y":3975,"width":280,"height":110},
		{"id":"c33b24452c95963b","type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> • These Perfect abilities may be sourced from different Perfect Sin types.","x":11723,"y":4207,"width":274,"height":135},
		{"id":"9e11536b838cfd02","x":12076,"y":4074,"width":406,"height":267,"type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> • If a Sin has a Perfect ability relating to its Severe Attack that references another Attack that its type has (e.g. an ability referencing Judgement on a HOUND Sin), that means this Sin can use ==either Severe Attack== when it has the opportunity. This does not give it more uses of its Severe attack (it must choose which one to use)."},
		{"id":"bffe58e62d04546c","x":12670,"y":3963,"width":330,"height":245,"type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> • Instead of gaining the special Complications related to Perfect Sins at the start of combat and at 50% talisman, the Sin starts with a number of these Complications active determined by how far Pressure has increased."},
		{"id":"347f775c61493c33","x":12681,"y":4300,"width":308,"height":169,"type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> • Other Perfect Sin rules (longer Execution talisman for traces and for itself) remain in effect as normal."},
		{"id":"fc16c30193dac8c1","x":12920,"y":3481,"width":250,"height":60,"type":"text","text":"==EMERGENCY TIMING=="},
		{"id":"260b283f362c90f3","x":13380,"y":3431,"width":370,"height":160,"type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> As the Fatal Decre Hunts are defined by something (or a lot) not having gone as planned, the Pressure talisman for these hunts is shorter."},
		{"id":"927c622f07ea08a1","x":13380,"y":3160,"width":369,"height":140,"type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> ***A Fatal Decree’s Pressure talisman can only take three slashes before the situation spirals out of control.***"},
		{"id":"df038054c8a55395","x":12865,"y":3160,"width":360,"height":137,"type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> That means Exorcists will have to choose very carefully what actions they take during the hunt."},
		{"id":"309a6dc95e4afd98","x":13988,"y":3480,"width":250,"height":60,"type":"text","text":"==TRAUMA QUESTIONS=="},
		{"id":"5416e33e617ea751","x":14460,"y":3412,"width":424,"height":199,"type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> As a Fatal Decree isn’t centred around investigation during the mission itself, it’s expected that players try to use their knowledge about the Pillar from prior in the campaign to answer the Sin’s Trauma Questions."},
		{"id":"568c9022aac6964e","x":14501,"y":3760,"width":342,"height":234,"type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> In addition, just before the FD, player Exorcists can carry out a ==Fatal Inquiry==, a special downtime internal investigation into their briefing documents and the records of CAIN to learn what they can about the Pillar."},
		{"id":"db4d28083c020bcd","x":14040,"y":3847,"width":250,"height":60,"type":"text","text":"Activity details present in page 54!"},
		{"id":"9508179dff6cae27","x":15121,"y":3481,"width":250,"height":60,"type":"text","text":"==REMAINING LOYALTY=="},
		{"id":"a2f1857950da6a17","x":15580,"y":3402,"width":418,"height":219,"type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> If players start a Fatal Decree while still having Loyalty tokens for that Pillar which they didn’t use during the Fatal Inquiry, they may use them as “mini”-Trauma Question answers, representing their familiarity with the Pillar’s abilities, temperament and history"},
		{"id":"0a5a855e4269d52a","x":15610,"y":3040,"width":359,"height":227,"type":"text","text":"> [!NOTE]\n> During a Fatal Decree, players may spend Loyalty tokens of the appropriate Pillar to either ==negate a single stress== dealt by one of the Sin’s attacks or abilities, or they may convert them into ==pathos==."}
	],
